 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources. The method avoids the computational cost of neighbor tracking and surface reconnection required by other multiresolution methods [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources. No additional memory is required for connectivity graphs or neighbor lists [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** The method achieves high interactive frame rates, especially in dynamic sculpting sessions where the LOD changes frequently [S1]. It avoids the slowdown associated with neighbor tracking in unconstrained hierarchies [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** High performance; simplicity of implementation; suitability for interactive applications where visual perfection is secondary to responsiveness [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Non-watertight output; visible cracks between cells of different resolutions; not suitable for applications requiring strict geometric continuity [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Interactive volume sculpting systems, real-time visualization where performance is critical, and scenarios where cracks are visually negligible due to high polygon density [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** Applications requiring watertight meshes for simulation, manufacturing, or precise geometric analysis [S1].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of active cells, as each cell is processed independently without neighbor lookups or global connectivity checks [S1].
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for storing the independent surface polygons, with no additional overhead for connectivity graphs or neighbor maps [S1].
+- **Measured Performance:** Enables interactive frame rates during dynamic LOD selection in resolution adaptive volume sculpting systems [S1].
+- **Strengths:** Avoids the computational cost of neighbor tracking and surface reconnection; maintains high display rates even in unconstrained hierarchies [S1].
+- **Limitations:** Visible cracks may appear between cells of different resolutions; not suitable for applications requiring watertight meshes or precise geometric continuity [S1].
+- **When to Use:** Interactive visualization and sculpting where frame rate is critical and minor visual artifacts are acceptable [S1].
+- **When Not to Use:** Applications requiring exportable, watertight meshes or high-fidelity static rendering where cracks are unacceptable [S1].
 
 ## Background
-The method was introduced by Eric Ferley, Marie-Paule Cani, and Jean-François Dufour in their 2001 paper "Resolution Adaptive Volume Sculpting" [S1]. It was developed to support an interactive volume sculpting system that allows users to manipulate 3D shapes using a mouse or space mouse metaphor [S1]. The approach is part of a broader family of multiresolution isosurfacing techniques that balance accuracy and performance [G].
+The method was introduced in the context of resolution adaptive volume sculpting by Eric Ferley, Marie-Paule Cani, and Jean-François Dufour in 2001 [S1]. It was designed to support interactive sculpting with a "virtual trackball" metaphor, allowing users to dynamically adjust resolution levels without being hindered by the computational cost of maintaining mesh connectivity [S1]. The approach contrasts with traditional multiresolution isosurfacing methods that prioritize geometric correctness over interactive performance [S1].
 
